  "slug": "artist:joao-gilberto",
  "name": "João Gilberto",
  "editorial_article": "João Gilberto, the Brazilian guitarist and singer born in Juazeiro in 1931, revolutionized the landscape of popular music in the late 1950s by pioneering the bossa nova beat. His innovations included recording with two separate microphones—one dedicated to his voice and one for his guitar—allowing for a more balanced, internalized sound that prioritized natural volume over traditional projection. This technique, developed alongside composer and arranger Antônio Carlos Jobim, was essential for the rhythmic complexity of tracks like 'Chega de Saudade' and 'Bim Bom,' both released on his 1958 Odeon debut. Gilberto’s influence extended globally through the 1964 album 'Getz/Gilberto,' a collaboration with jazz saxophonist Stan Getz that famously became the first jazz record to win the Grammy Award for Album of the Year. His vocal delivery, marked by a laid-back, hushed intimacy, was described by critic Chris Richards as a place where human breath transformed into music. Throughout his long career, which included a 2001 Grammy for his studio album 'João voz e violão,' he remained a foundational figure whose syncopated guitar style drew as much from traditional samba as it did from contemporary jazz harmonies.",
